//! Multiplayer in lockstep (`docs/multiplayer.md`). A host and up to six guests play a generated
//! world, each on their own side, and the AI plays the rest. Every machine runs the whole game.
//! Turns are simultaneous, so a player's planning stays on their own machine until they end it:
//! then their side's *plan* (`plan.tua`: its units' orders, its cities' queues and citizens, its
//! placed jobs, its stockpile) is this machine's lockstep input for the turn. Once the host's
//! sequencer holds every seat's input it sends every machine the same bundle of them, and each
//! machine applies the plans, in side order, to the game as it stood when the turn's planning began
//! (`turn_start`) and resolves the turn. The same plans on the same game resolve the same way (the
//! simulation is deterministic), and a checksum of the result, compared after every turn, catches
//! it if they ever don't.
//!
//! This module is the game's side of the protocol: what a plan holds, whether one is sound, and
//! how a bundle of them becomes a turn. `link/` moves the messages (`lockstep`, sealed by `seal`).
//! Every machine, the host's included, applies bundles the same way, so a plan that fails the
//! checks is dropped identically everywhere and the host then drops the player that sent it.

